when night falls in southern portugal  it's time for mauricio nogueira to head  out to sea  the fisherman is heading for his fishing  grounds two nautical miles off the coast  of villarreal to santo antonio  there he will catch octopus like many  here in the algarve  my great grandfather grandfather and  father fished octopus as well  and my brothers too you need to have it  in your blood  it's a tough profession the ocean takes  everything out of you  strong winds and high waves make the  work difficult tonight  the 44 year old fisherman is committed  to the traditional way of catching  octopus  he sinks clay pots into the sea with a  rope and uses a winch to lift the pots  out of the water  in sum there will be an octopus hiding  this way of fishing is just fairer the  octopus goes into the clay pot to sleep  and can swim out at any time  fish traps on the other hand are just  traps  but fish traps are being used more and  more often  mauricio believes this is one of the  reasons why there are fewer and fewer  octopuses in the region  40 years ago there were no traps just  clay pots  my father tells me that in those days  they were octopi  all year round today more and more clay  pots are being replaced with traps  and some nights i hardly catch anything  [Music]  santa lucia is the algarves octopus  fishing hub  and the fishermen know all the fishing  methods inside out  joaquin oliveira has opted for traps  with bait inside  the 57 year old says it's more efficient  and convenient  because the traps can also be retrieved  during the day  the octopus goes in here and can't get  out  one meal until 2000 i also worked with  clay potts  then i switched to fish traps because it  makes for less hard work  fishing is easier and you just catch  more  [Music]  mauritsu can hardly keep up with the  fisherman's yields from their traps  even tonight his catch was small  compared to theirs  just 50 octopus or 70 kilos  yet the demand is on the rise year for  year  but octopus fishing is changing there  are more traps  and more plastic the traditional clay  pots are being replaced with plastic  containers  is a marine biologist at algarve  university  she's worried about this development and  points out that the oceans are already  full of microparticles  she tries to promote sustainability and  has one project with local fishermen  around 27 of all plastic that  is in the sea comes from lost gears or  or fishing devices so  there's there's there's a huge problem  there with those  these spots and with other fishing gears  that get lost and they're made of  plastic material  mauricio goes out fishing again the next  night  he has to hurry up to get the last  octopi before they wake up and swim out  of the containers  and he also once again has to repair a  rope that's been cut  deliberately the rope is broken because  another fisherman cut it  they don't respect my space i occupy a  large area with my clay pots  and that annoys the others  there is a lot of competition and it's  tough for him on the market and at sea  but maurizio wants to keep to his old  methods  even if that means he has to stay out  until the early morning hours  if you work hard and you're skillful you  can earn decent money in a month  you have to put up with the cold and the  bad weather  but you can make a pretty good living  he believes that his method is more  sustainable and hopes that it will one  day be required in the algarve  so that the sea is protected  you
 

